### Drill 5: CoreDNS Diagnostics & Recovery (`05-coredns-recovery/`)

Cluster-wide DNS failures cause applications to fail with `cannot resolve host` or connection timeouts when resolving
service names (e.g. `service.namespace.svc.cluster.local`).

#### Step 1: Simulate CoreDNS Syntax Error

```bash
cd {{ cka_lab_cluster_troubleshooting_dir }}/05-coredns-recovery
./break-coredns.sh
```

#### Step 2: Observe DNS Resolution Failure

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
```

Notice that CoreDNS pods are restarting or reporting errors. Launch a test query:

```bash
kubectl run dns-test --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never --rm -i -- nslookup kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local || echo "DNS query failed!"
```

#### Step 3: Inspect CoreDNS Logs

```bash
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns --tail=30
```

Observe Corefile parsing errors or forwarder syntax issues.

#### Step 4: Repair the Corefile ConfigMap

CoreDNS configuration is stored in the `coredns` ConfigMap in `kube-system`:

```bash
# Inspect the live configuration
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml

# Edit or restore the clean Corefile
./fix-coredns.sh

# Restart the deployment to pick up the corrected ConfigMap
kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system
kubectl rollout status deployment coredns -n kube-system --timeout=60s
```

#### Step 5: Verify DNS Resolution

```bash
./test-dns-resolution.sh
```

